  "text": "**Solution 1 (α = −1)** is not credible. Its survival recursion is a mean-field heuristic that treats each scale's destruction hazard as independent of the loop structure. That gives a clean 1/(c log n) survival probability, but it ignores the intersection-geometry effects that drive the known logarithmic corrections in d = 4. It also gives no support for the exponent −1 beyond its own algebra.\n\n**Solution 2 (α = −1/3)** rests on a known result: Lawler's theorem that the chronological loop-erased random walk in Z⁴ has E|LE(S[0,n])| = n(log n)^{−1/3+o(1)}. Its weak point is the step that transfers this exponent to the LLF procedure. The claim that both procedures \"process the same total loop measure\" so the exponent must match is asserted rather than derived. The LLF exponent could differ from the chronological one, so this is not verified.\n\nI have no tools here to check the literature, so I can't confirm the LLF exponent directly. On the evidence available, Solution 2 is the more plausible of the two, and Solution 1's answer looks like an artifact of its heuristic.\n\nCHOICE: 2",
